" IDE-like vimrc for gscode3 two-column diff editor.
" Left window: read-only HEAD version. Right window: working tree (editable).

set nocompatible
set mouse=a
set number
set ruler
set laststatus=2
set scrollbind
set cursorline
set hlsearch
syntax on

" Arrow keys, PageUp/PageDown, Home/End all work natively in both modes.
" Make insert mode behave more like a regular editor:
"   - Backspace works across line boundaries
"   - Shift+arrows select text (visual mode)
set backspace=indent,eol,start
set selectmode=mouse,key
set keymodel=startsel,stopsel
set selection=inclusive

" Sync vim's yank/paste with the macOS system clipboard so that:
"   - Selecting text with Shift+arrows + pressing y (or just deleting it)
"     puts the text on the system clipboard automatically.
"   - Cmd-V in any other app pastes vim's yanked content.
" Requires vim built with +clipboard (stock macOS Homebrew vim has it).
if has('clipboard')
    set clipboard=unnamed,unnamedplus
endif

" macOS clipboard workflow:
"   COPY  → select with Shift+arrows, press `y`. With clipboard=unnamed above,
"           it lands on the system clipboard. Then Cmd-V works in any app.
"   PASTE → in insert mode, just press Cmd-V — the terminal sends the
"           clipboard text directly as keystrokes. (Vim's `p` also works in
"           normal mode and pulls from the system clipboard.)

" Lock the left (HEAD) buffer as read-only.
augroup gscode3_lock_left
    autocmd!
    autocmd VimEnter * if winnr('$') >= 2 | 1wincmd w | setlocal readonly nomodifiable | 2wincmd w | endif
augroup END

" IDE-style save / quit bindings.
" The wrapper script runs `stty -ixon susp undef` so Ctrl-S / Ctrl-X reach vim
" instead of getting eaten by the terminal (flow-control / suspend).
"
" Ctrl-S saves the editable file.
nnoremap <silent> <C-s> :w<CR>
inoremap <silent> <C-s> <C-o>:w<CR>
vnoremap <silent> <C-s> <C-c>:w<CR>

" Ctrl-X quits everything (asks if unsaved). Chosen because Ctrl-Q is
" historically captured by terminal flow control and many macOS configs.
nnoremap <silent> <C-x> :qa<CR>
inoremap <silent> <C-x> <C-o>:qa<CR>

" Alt-Down / Alt-Up jump to the next / previous diff hunk (IDE-style
" alternative to vim's `]c` / `[c`).
nnoremap <silent> <M-Down> ]czz
nnoremap <silent> <M-Up>   [czz
inoremap <silent> <M-Down> <C-o>]c<C-o>zz
inoremap <silent> <M-Up>   <C-o>[c<C-o>zz

" Make the editable window (right) the active one on start, and bounce back
" if anything (mouse click, Ctrl-W, etc.) tries to focus the read-only pane.
autocmd VimEnter * 2wincmd w
augroup gscode3_stay_right
    autocmd!
    autocmd WinEnter * if winnr() == 1 && winnr('$') >= 2 | 2wincmd w | endif
augroup END

" Disable vim's built-in window-switching shortcuts so the user can't get
" stuck in the read-only HEAD pane.
nnoremap <C-w> <Nop>
inoremap <C-w> <Nop>

" Friendly status line hint.
autocmd VimEnter * echo "[gscode3] Type: i   Stop typing: Esc   Save: Ctrl-S   Quit: Ctrl-X   (gscode3 --help for full cheatsheet)"
